1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070648/state.json" "file_sha256" "16453f15dc6e978b770a1cf08c037485c2e01bb0d27f6535e75b7cc73edc41d9"} {"question" "q-333863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070648/state.json" "file_sha256" "16453f15dc6e978b770a1cf08c037485c2e01bb0d27f6535e75b7cc73edc41d9"} {"question" "q-333863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "87a024714285f995a264000b8d4ee3dead3fef4b34be37c1ece7359b2ed39565"} {"question" "q-333863" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070648/state.json" "file_sha256" "16453f15dc6e978b770a1cf08c037485c2e01bb0d27f6535e75b7cc73edc41d9"} {"question" "q-333863" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070648/state.json" "file_sha256" "16453f15dc6e978b770a1cf08c037485c2e01bb0d27f6535e75b7cc73edc41d9"} {"question" "q-333863" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070648/state.json" "file_sha256" "16453f15dc6e978b770a1cf08c037485c2e01bb0d27f6535e75b7cc73edc41d9"} {"question" "q-333863" 